Body:
There’s a working alternate path for this while the OpenCode replay bug is unresolved.

I maintain [Swobu](https://github.com/swobuforge/swobu), a local compatibility gateway. Swobu terminates the client-side protocol loop and sends the request to DeepSeek through its native Messages endpoint, so OpenCode does not need to reconstruct DeepSeek’s Chat `reasoning_content` history.

I tested the full path with thinking enabled: OpenCode read files, edited the source, ran `go test`, and continued successfully across multiple tool results.

Install the current release:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://swobu.com/install.sh | sh
swobu
```

Add a DeepSeek target using `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`, then configure OpenCode:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "swobu/default",
  "provider": {
    "swobu": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Swobu",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:7926/c/dev/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "default": {
          "name": "Default"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

<img width="1700" height="1232" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/eae5c580-fa58-4916-a877-647da09d87d7" />

This can also be worked around by moving the provider credential boundary out of OpenCode rather than duplicating the key in its picker.

I maintain [Swobu](https://swobu.com/), a local compatibility gateway. OpenCode connects to a local OpenAI-compatible workspace with a non-secret placeholder, while the real Z.AI Coding Plan or OpenRouter credential stays in Swobu:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://swobu.com/install.sh | sh
swobu
```

After adding the target in Cockpit, configure OpenCode with a custom provider:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "swobu/zai",
  "provider": {
    "swobu": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Swobu",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:7926/c/dev/v1",
        "apiKey": "unused"
      },
      "models": {
        "zai": {
          "name": "Z.AI"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

<img width="1432" height="1250" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/47f77ea3-4c3c-452a-8d70-2a1930e1b873" />

That bypasses the affected built-in provider credential lookup entirely: the picker only sees `swobu/zai`, and OpenCode never needs the real provider key. This is a workaround for the credential boundary, not a fix for the `auth.json`/TUI state mismatch described here.

The isolation here points to the hosted Flash lane rather than the OpenCode client. If you have direct DeepSeek API access, you can keep using OpenCode while bypassing that lane.

I maintain [Swobu](https://swobu.com/), a local compatibility gateway:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://swobu.com/install.sh | sh
swobu
```

Add a DeepSeek target in Cockpit using your `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` and `deepseek-v4-flash`, then point OpenCode at the local workspace:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "swobu/deepseek-v4-flash",
  "provider": {
    "swobu": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Swobu",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:7926/c/dev/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "deepseek-v4-flash": {
          "name": "DeepSeek V4 Flash"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This keeps OpenCode as the client but sends requests to your direct DeepSeek target instead of `https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1`.

It does not restore the OpenCode Go Flash lane or transfer its plan entitlement; it requires separate direct DeepSeek API access to that model.
